-- title:
--   Proposition 4.1(ii): every subsequential weak limit gives a weak solution of (SDIR) with data (2.17)
-- statement:
--   Consider a sequence of load-balancing systems satisfying (2.9)–(2.13), and assume (IC₀). Let $(X,L,E,S,P)$ be a subsequential weak limit of $(\hat X^n,\hat L^n,\hat E^n,\hat S^n,\hat P^{\#,n})$ along a subsequence $n_k$, realised on a probability space $(\Omega',\mathcal F',P')$ with continuous sample paths. Let
--
--   $$
--   B_i=\sigma_i^{-1}(E_i-S_i),\qquad \sigma_i=(\lambda_i+\mu_i(\sigma^{ser}_i)^2)^{1/2}.
--   $$
--
--   Then there are a filtration $\mathbb F$ on $\Omega'$ and an $\mathbb F$-progressively measurable process $\beta$ with the following properties. Almost surely, for a.e. $t\ge0$, $\beta(t)$ is the derivative of $P$ at $t$. And $(X,L,B)$, with rank-dependent drift $\beta$, forms a weak solution of (SDIR) with the data (2.17), $b=\lambda_0p$, $m=\hat\lambda-\hat\mu$.
--
--   This identifies the limit points of the queueing model as solutions of the rank-based differential inclusion with reflection.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The following conventions are fixed.
--
--   - The subsequence is a strictly increasing $\varphi:\mathbb N\to\mathbb N$, and the convergence is the referenced `CouplingConverges`.
--   - The limit is assumed to have a.s. continuous paths, which is how a $D$-valued random element supported on $C$ is represented. The conclusion is stated up to indistinguishability: there are processes $\tilde X,\tilde L,\tilde B$, a.s. equal to $X,L,B$ at all $t\ge0$, that form the weak solution. This is needed because a weak solution's paths are required to be continuous, nonnegative, and so on, for every $\omega$.
--   - The paper does not name the filtration. It is existential here; the natural candidate is the augmentation of the filtration generated by $(X,L,B,P)$.
-- source:
--   Atar & Ichiba, Rank-Based Stochastic Differential Inclusions and Diffusion Limits for a Load Balancing Model, arXiv:2409.15121v1, p. 11, Proposition 4.1(ii) (data from Theorem 2.3, p. 8, (2.17); standing assumption (IC₀) of §4.1)

